{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["R6", "U2", "U3"],
      "pins": ["R6.1", "R6.2", "U2.2", "U3.2", "U2.8", "U3.8"],
      "nets": ["TACH", "VCC", "+5V"],
      "observed": "The 10k pull-up R6 on the TACH net is tied to the global rail VCC (the unregulated input rail that feeds the LM1117-5.0 input pin and the high-side PMOS fan switch), while the same TACH net drives the MCU tachometer input pin2 (PB4) of U2/U3, whose VCC pin8 is on the regulated +5V rail.",
      "expected": "A pull-up for a signal that is read by the ATtiny must be tied to the MCU's own supply (+5V), exactly as the RST pull-up R3 is correctly tied to +5V. Pulling TACH to VCC (which is necessarily higher than +5V, otherwise the 5V regulator could not function) places an over-voltage on the MCU input pin.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "R6 = 10K, top pin to global label 'VCC', bottom pin to label 'TACH' (sheet /, row A)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U2 ATtiny45-20P pin2 = 'TACH', pin8 = '+5V'; U3 ATtiny13V-10S pin2 = 'TACH', pin8 = '+5V' (sheet /, rows B/C)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "RST pull-up R3 = 10K tied to '+5V' (correct rail), whereas R6 tied to 'VCC' (sheet /, row A)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U1 LM1117-5.0 pin2 = 'VCC' (input), pin3 = '+5V' (output) (sheet /, row A) - proves VCC > +5V"}
      ],
      "impact": "When the fan tach transistor is off, the TACH line is pulled to VCC (e.g. 12V), exceeding the ATtiny pin absolute-maximum (VCC+0.5V) and the +5V rail; this risks damaging/latching-up the MCU input and gives a wrong logic level on the tachometer reading.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The fan driver, regulator and ISP wiring are internally consistent (VCC is the high switched rail, +5V is the regulated MCU rail), but the TACH pull-up R6 is connected to VCC instead of the MCU's +5V rail, over-volting the ATtiny tachometer input; this is the deviation from intended design."
}

